          "number": 2,
          "letter": "B",
          "english": "The most dangerous thing for these remote island dwellers is land erosion. The second is lack of access to medical supplies and doctors. There are no doctors within miles, and while child mortality and maternal death have gone down in the rest of the country, this is not the case for the islands. The medical situation is so bad that it really takes away from the quality of their life. Yet for many island inhabitants—some of Bangladesh's poorest—paying for health care is a costly ordeal. Victims of erosion lose their houses, agricultural land and jobs as farmers, fishermen and day laborers. Though government hospitals are free, many people hesitate to go, citing long commutes, endless lines and questionable diagnoses. For convenience's sake, one-third of rural households visit unqualified village doctors, who rely on unscientific methods of treatment, according to a 2016 study in the peer-reviewed journal Global Health Action.",
          "chinese": "对于这些偏远岛屿的居民来说，最危险的事情是土地侵蚀。第二个问题是无法获得医疗用品和医生。方圆数英里内都没有医生；尽管该国其他地区的儿童死亡率和孕产妇死亡率都已下降，但岛上并非如此。岛上的医疗状况非常糟糕，严重影响了居民的生活质量。然而，对许多岛民来说——其中一些是孟加拉国最贫困的人——支付医疗费用是一件代价高昂的难事。土地侵蚀的受害者会失去房屋、农田以及作为农民、渔民和临时工的工作。虽然公立医院提供免费医疗，但许多人仍不愿前往，理由是路途遥远、排队无休无止以及诊断结果令人存疑。根据同行评议期刊《全球健康行动》2016年的一项研究，为了方便起见，三分之一的农村家庭会去找没有资质的乡村医生，而这些医生依靠不科学的方法进行治疗。",
